# SELFBOOT -- RealXV6 changes its own kernel and boots the result.
#
#     /bin/mount /dev/rk1 /src
#     drun /src/sys/selfboot
#
# mkkern proves the target rebuilds its kernel byte-identical to the
# golden.  That is reproduction; this is authorship.  The banner in
# KEN/MAIN.C is edited HERE, by ed, on the machine itself; the kernel is
# recompiled, folded into a .COM, laid onto a 360K floppy image, written
# to the medium the machine boots from, and the machine is reset.  What
# comes back prints the new string, and nothing outside this disk was
# involved in any step of it.
#
# So this deliberately does NOT dcmp against /test/REFK.COM.  It cannot:
# the source no longer matches the golden, which is the entire point.
# mkkern is the run that checks the toolchain reproduces; this is the run
# that checks the system can be changed.
#
# RUN mkkern FIRST.  After ed, only MAIN.O is stale, so this recompiles
# that one source and relinks -- a minute rather than the half hour 32
# compiles take.  Without mkkern's objects the dbind below fails to open
# the first .O and drun stops there.
#
# ed's exit status is no use as a check: a substitute that matches
# nothing prints '?' and ed carries on, writes the file back unchanged
# and quits happily.  So grep is the guard -- it exits nonzero when the
# new string is absent, which stops the run before a kernel that was
# never edited gets written to the boot floppy.
#
# The boot sector is assembled here as well, and dcmp'd against the
# vendored BOOT.COM before anything is written.  Getting that wrong would
# not fail loudly: it would produce a floppy the BIOS cannot boot, and
# the only symptom would be a machine that never comes back.
#
# The image is built as a file and copied to /dev/fd0 rather than written
# to the device directly: mkboot ends by reading its output back and
# checking there is nothing beyond it, and on a block special that read
# hits the end of the medium as an error rather than as EOF.  cp needs no
# special case -- creat() on an existing special file skips itrunc, so
# the node keeps its major/minor and the write goes through writei's
# IFBLK path straight to fdstrategy.
#
# reboot(2) is what makes the result survivable: it flushes the buffer
# cache and waits for the disk inside the kernel, then pulses the 8042
# reset line.  See RT/REBOOT.C for why a user-mode sync() would not do.

cd /src/sys

# --- edit the kernel's banner, on the machine, with the machine's editor
ed KEN/MAIN.C <banner.ed
grep DeSmet KEN/MAIN.C

# --- rebuild: only MAIN.C changed
c88 KEN/MAIN -iH/
dbind -fKERNL
e2c UNIX.EXE UNIX.COM UNIX.MAP

# --- assemble the boot sector and check it against the vendored one
asm88 BOOT/BOOTSEC
dbind -fBOOTL
mkboot -s BOOTSEC.EXE BOOTSEC.COM
dcmp BOOTSEC.COM BOOT.COM

# --- lay the floppy down and put it on the medium
mkboot BOOTSEC.COM UNIX.COM UNIX360.IMG
cp UNIX360.IMG /dev/fd0
echo === SELFBOOT WRITTEN ===

# --- flush, reset, and come back up on what we just wrote
reboot
